 second thing we did was we  froze all transfers of police from  station to station so any police officer  there was no police station had to stay  there for  well up to two years and this was  because as of now and actually in  Rajasthan in all of India transfers are  used as a way of kind of rewarding  favorite favorites and punishing people  who are kind of down in the police  through those kind of backdoor channel  so there's some locations that are  better like than others maybe it's in a  big city or maybe it's in an area which  has a lot of potential for collecting  corruption money sure and those are used  as a reward whereas other ones in rural  areas or where there's less corruption  potential are used as punishment so what  we try to do is eliminate that channel  by stopping all transfers for two years  in addition to that we implanted some  more stuff within the police stations  that were a little similar so we also  made a duty rotation system within the  police station and attach that to a 24  of the 24 hour break every week so  actually as of in fact as of now  currently the police in India don't ever  get a day off and are on duty 24 hours a  day oh my goodness so we wanted to test  and see you know if we actually scale it  down a little bit that has made them  work only six days a week rather than  seven days a week would they be more  productive for the remaining six days a  week and also we rotated the duties they  did within the station and finally we  did a fourth intervention which was a  community observer so the idea was that  it every day in the month a new person  from the community would come in and  just sit in the police station for three  hours and they weren't supposed to you  know do any work or do anything like  that they were just supposed to see how  the police operated and the idea there  was sort of twofold on the one hand we  wanted to give some members of the  community especially more influential  members like the local high school  principal or local doctors that kind of  thing a sort of some insight into the  house police did their job in non crisis  situations because we felt that most  people only saw the police when  something was bad like they were getting  arrested or there was a crime or  something like that on the other hand we  also wanted to ensure that for at least  a few hours in the day other potential  crime victims are that kind of thing  could come into the police station and  know that there was another member of  the community there so if there for  example intimidated by the police or  thought they were being forced to pay a  bribe they could go during this  three-hour period when there was a  community observer there and know that  they wouldn't be  alone in the police station those were  the four different interventions that we  that we experiment okay you also as part  of your study conducted a crime survey  which my understanding is it's the first  that's been that was ever done in India  talk a little bit about that yeah that's  right so in places like the US or Europe  or really almost all developed countries  crime is measured primarily through a  household survey so many people out  there in America maybe not us but some  people are going to interviewed every  year about whether there are victims of  crimes in India that's not the case  ninja crime is measured by the number of  crimes that are reported to the police  so this leads to kind of a perverse  situation where for a certain police  officer or even a politician wants to  reduce crime all they have to do is stop  actually registering the cases in their  log books and crime is automatically  lower in fact it's even more perverse  than that because if for example the  local police doesn't seem very friendly  to the population or it's intimidating  or that kind of thing local population  may just not even bother to report a  crime and it will seem as if crime is  lower in that case as well so because of  this perverse incentive the whole crime  reporting system in India is actually  encouraging the police to do their jobs  in the bad way so what we wanted to do  was pilot a system where we would  actually collect data on crime in India  in a way that was much more similar to  the way it's collected us actually a  house-by-house survey on whether people  had been a victim of a crime okay did  you was this something we've done  through the mail or did you visit  household no we actually hired a lot of  people who went literally door-to-door  to randomly selected households as is  done in the US and asked people whether  they had been a victim of one of many  different types of crimes right so what  were what were some of your findings of  the study so well we found several  different things first of all the crime  the crime interview found the crime  survey found that indeed crabs were very  largely under reported in India and  different categories different amounts  of random reporting and what we found in  the effect of our interventions was  actually very interesting so some of  them didn't work at all the bringing  them remember the community didn't work  at all the weekly day off and Duty  rotation no effect we did find some  small effects of the freeze on transfer  and the training particularly on  people's reported fear of the police and  also on how satisfied crime victims were  with how the police have dealt with  their cases but i think the real sort of  broad takeaway of this was that those  the two interventions that did work that  is the freeze on transfers in the  training or ones that could be  implemented much more from the top down  on the other hand the others the the  community observers and the weekly  offered the Duty rotation required the  head of the police station the police  chief at that individual station to kind  of buy-in and what we found was that  there was people the local police chiefs  were very resistant to the whole program  because they saw that they didn't feel  that there was anything in for them and  they also felt that they would be  transferred very soon maybe you know in  a year so they didn't believe in the  freeze on transfers they thought they'd  be transferred very soon and they didn't  see that there was any need to there's  any gain for them for investing serving  the long-term relationship with the  community or the long-term morale of the  other police staff in that police  station by implementing these programs  because they felt they would just leave  very soon right right well that doesn't  bode very well for the community I would  imagine then unfortunately not all right  right okay let's talk about another  study you've done about deterring  drugged driving driving in the same area  of Rajasthan and I'm curious did this  study grow out of the work you did  initially with the police reform yes it  absolutely did so our first police  reform project was very broad we were  trying to you know broadly reform the  whole relationship of the police with  the community and as I mentioned you  know we were only partially successful  in that so for a second work with the  police I think both we and the police  felt that it was time to focus on  something much more quantifiable and  direct so we chose drunken driving which  was a priority of the police at that  time and in particular one of the things  we did in the second study that built  off of what we learned about the  importance of motivating the staff is  particularly to lower levels was to try  experimenting with giving some police  staff extra incentives to actually do  the sobriety checkpoints that they were  assigned so in fact we learned from the  experience that really what the radisson  police value is a good transfer so that  we took some police and we offered them  a transfer to a more desirable location  if they performed well in forcing  drunken revelry to actually try to use  that perverse incentive in as a sort of  as a carrot to surprise yeah well we  don't tell it that yes and and part of  this was also a first because it's the  first time you conducted randomized  evaluations of sobriety checkpoints and  it's one of it's one of the only  policing experiment experiments in the  developing world so you know you're  doing a lot of new stuff over there  which i think is great so you know kind  of give us an overview how did how did  the study work yeah so that yeah I mean  more broadly you know these police  experiments have been done in the US but  sort of at a very slow rate and actually  much more in the 60s and 70s so we're  kind of taking that literature and  applying it to India but also extending  it a lot and you know what we found on  the on the road blocks was one of the  things we were testing was whether it's  better to have surprise roadblocks so  where the population doesn't know where  the police are going to do it or to  always have a roadblock at say the most  dangerous location may be right outside  the area where all the bars are that  kind of thing you could imagine you know  that really if you want to stop people  it's obvious we should put the roadblock  and everybody expects her to be a  roadblock there no one will drive drunk  on that section of road so what we found  was actually that the surprise  checkpoints were more effective the pink  Putin people on their toes works better  and in particular we actually were able  to document or quantify the effect that  if you put up a fixed roadblock it  becomes less effective over time because  people actually learn where it is  whereas a surprise road blocks where  didn't we didn't see the kind of  learning effect there we managed to keep  people sufficiently surprised that they  didn't figure out you know where to go  good boy yeah that makes sense to me but  curiously and this in this country you  know I often read in newspapers around  holiday times that there's going to be  you know a checkpoint at such and such a  location and that always I always  wondered why they do that I think  probably has to do with  some kind of legal issues would be my  guess but that wasn't wasn't necessarily  the case in India well actually  different countries do a different ways  so a particularly interesting example is  the French who initially made the  locations of their radar guns secret and  then told everyone where the radar guns  were in order to make them apprentice  was speeding at those exact points and  then made them secret again so this is  clearly kind of a policy question that  different police departments haven't  really figured out is whether you should  go surprise or fix that you know it  seems like some departments in us as you  suggested are moving in the more fixed  direction but certainly for enforcing  speed on highways you know in the US  Highway Patrol's have very much taken up  the surprise sure right exactly so what  can we take away from this drunk driving  study well I mean I think there that  really you know the surprise approach is  more effective and another thing that we  did was we also like i mentioned  compared these police staff that we had  incentivized with the promise of you  know future good postings with just  normal police staff who had no greater  incentive to work then the police  normally do have and there we found that  indeed the incentivized a for doing much  much better i think thats gets at a much  broader issue in governance in  developing countries which is that  there's very rarely individual  incentives either for government  bureaucrats and developing countries or  for police certainly never the types of  attentive you would see in like a firm  in the US or something where you know  your bonus depends exactly on your  performance and I think incorporating  those even in a relatively mild way  through the offer of future positive  transfers at a very substantial effect  on outcomes I think that's something  that could be generalized to much more  areas of grammar that's post in  developing it do you think based on the  two studies that you've done they will  perhaps begin to incorporate more more  carrots so to speak in terms of getting  the police to do certain things yeah  absolutely we hope so I mean India is  now India has never had a dedicated  highway patrol on there now setting one  up and we're in contact with the people  who are you know developing that in  developing the institution of the  Highway Patrol  and they're very interested in this  research and trying to build a high  building some of the some of the results  of this research into the highway did  they contact you because they knew about  the studies yeah so we we now have many  connections within the police in India  and they have heard about it through  that excellent um did you encounter any  surprises in either study that you  weren't anticipating or expected I think  you know this the surprise was how  similar the police is to say a firm in  the US in many ways you know we expected  that the police would work in a very  military militaristic structure where  the incentives or motivations of the  lower down the staff Lord down in the  ranks weren't that important for  functioning they would just follow  orders but what we found was indeed you  know their their incentives matter a lot  and keeping them making sure that  whatever project you do provides them  with some immediate rewards is very  important much more important than we  thought it was going to be when we  started out where we thought they would  just follow orders blend very  interesting thank you very much for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your work my pleasure happy to  talk about it for more information about  professor canesten and his research  please visit our website at yale edu  slash macmillan report be sure to join  us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Mac